      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(a) Dat. s. all end in -ι (whether subscript or not).\n(b) Dat. pls. all end in -ις or -σι(ν) (but note the exceptions: ἡμῖν, ὑμῖν.)\n(c) Type 3 nouns:\n(i) those with stems ending in -οντ- have dat. pl. in -ουσι(ν), e.g. participles like παύ-ων with stem παυοντ- produce the dat. pl. παύουσι(ν).*\n(ii) those in -σαντ- have dat. pl. in -σᾶσι(ν).\n(iii) those with a single consonant at the end of the stem either drop it in the dat. pl. (λιμήν, stem λιμεν-, dat. pl. λιμέσι) or let it coalesce with the σ of the ending (φύλαξ, stem φυλακ-, dat. pl. φύλαξι [= φύλακ-σι]).\nSee also 359.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "footnote",
      "order": 3,
      "bbox": [
        193,
        307,
        334,
        888
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "* Ouch! For the form παύουσι(ν) can be either 3rd pl. pres. indic. 'they stop' or a m./n. dat. pl. of the pres. participle! Only context will tell you which.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": "*",
